## One finding worth acting on

The MCP path **failed first** with the old error, then the rebuilt CLI succeeded. The running `ticket-mcp` server is serving **pre-fix code**:

> dependency not progressed: cannot move ticket 322a4737… to 'planned' because dependency 9590bdcf… is only at 'open'

So every agent using ticket-mcp will keep hitting the old guard until the server is restarted against a rebuilt binary. Nothing in the workflow signals this — an agent gets a plausible-looking domain error and has no way to tell it's stale-binary drift rather than real policy. That's a silent correctness trap for any MCP-served fix, not just this one.

Worth a ticket? It generalizes beyond this change — the 11 MCP servers in [.vscode/mcp.json](.vscode/mcp.json) all launch prebuilt binaries through `mcp-cost-gate`.

Also still open from earlier: `980cf1fa`'s condensed description (deliberate, `done` ticket) was left unrestored pending your call, and C1 (the routing table) is still the unblocked head of the consolidation epic.

Yes, you can add this functionality, but a standard MCP server cannot restart itself directly because it runs as a child process. When an MCP server exits, the host client (like VS Code or Claude Desktop) detects the broken standard input/output (stdio) stream and marks the server as disconnected rather than relaunching it. [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 
To make a working "restart button" tool, you must use one of the two architecture patterns detailed below.

Please investigate this and consider adding a toolmon wrapper for all of our mcp tools that handles binary reloads and restarts. Write it in rust.
---

## The Process Wrapper / Watcher (Recommended)
Instead of running your custom executable directly, you wrap it in a lightweight manager process. The host client talks to the wrapper via `stdio`. The wrapper then spins up your actual server executable as a grandchild process and pipes all messages through. [6, 7, 8] 
When the restart tool is called, the wrapper kills the grandchild process and launches a fresh executable without breaking the connection to the client. [6, 9] 

```
[ VS Code Client ] <--- stdio ---> [ Wrapper Process ] <--- internal pipe ---> [ Your Executable ]
```

## Example Implementation (Node.js/TypeScript)
You can build a wrapper directly into your tool or expose a custom tool that triggers the wrapper to swap the underlying process: [10] 

```
import { spawn } from 'child_process';
import { McpServer } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/mcp.js";
import { StdioServerTransport } from "@modelcontextprotocol/sdk/server/stdio.js";

let childProcess = launchExecutable();

function launchExecutable() {
  // Spin up your fresh binary or script
  const child = spawn('./my-fresh-executable', [], { stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'inherit'] });
  
  // Forward data from child back to VS Code via wrapper's stdout
  child.stdout.pipe(process.stdout);
  // Forward incoming VS Code stdin to the child
  process.stdin.pipe(child.stdin);
  
  return child;
}

// Inside your custom wrapper's MCP tool definition:
server.tool("restart_backend", {}, async () => {
  // 1. Unpipe streams to prevent memory leaks
  process.stdin.unpipe(childProcess.stdin);
  childProcess.stdout.unpipe(process.stdout);
  
  // 2. Kill the stale executable
  childProcess.kill('SIGTERM'); 
  
  // 3. Relaunch a fresh instance
  childProcess = launchExecutable();
  
  return { content: [{ type: "text", text: "Executable successfully recycled!" }] };
});
```

*Note: Existing community tools like [mcpmon](https://mcpservers.org/servers/neilopet/mcp-server-hmr) operate on this exact proxy principles to achieve live hot-reloading for custom servers.* [6, 11]

