PRIOR_ART_SUMMARY: The repo already centralizes viewer/server lifecycle around `viewer-ctl`: its spec (`server lifecycle`) documents `cargo install --path ... --force` and explicit stop/start semantics so a stale binary or running PID cannot block installs on Windows. A validation ticket shows a real Windows case: `d30e13e1` notes cargo install required stopping a stale PID (PID 8988) so the `.exe` lock was released. The `viewer-ctl` code implements PID discovery and robust kill semantics and on Windows spawns/detaches to avoid holding repository file handles.

VIEWER_CTL: viewer-api/viewer-ctl — a config-driven lifecycle manager (start/stop/install/restart/prepare). It enumerates PIDs on a port, kills them on stop, and on Windows spawns the server then exits to release file handles. Does it restart children? yes (it implements stop then start; `restart` calls `cmd_stop` then `cmd_start`).

